WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.694
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Introduction to
Algorithm Analysis as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.694 --> 00:00:10.046
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.046 --> 00:00:16.440
NARRATOR: Algorithm analysis connects a
precise problem specification to a correctness

4
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.765
argument and resource bounds.

5
00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:26.161
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A sorting
algorithm must guarantee that the output array

6
00:00:26.161 --> 00:00:29.859
contains all input elements in non-decreasing
order.

7
00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:36.698
NARRATOR: On screen, display a split screen
showing a problem statement on the left and a

8
00:00:36.698 --> 00:00:42.682
resource usage graph on the right, with arrows
linking them to indicate the analytical

9
00:00:42.682 --> 00:00:43.109
connection.

10
00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:49.050
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

11
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.421
change the conclusion.

12
00:00:50.421 --> 00:00:56.062
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

13
00:00:56.186 --> 00:01:02.611
NARRATOR: Now define Asymptotic Notation
Basics precisely enough that another learner

14
00:01:02.611 --> 00:01:04.948
could test the definition.

15
00:01:04.948 --> 00:01:11.604
NARRATOR: Asymptotic notation describes how
resource use grows with input size.

16
00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:19.646
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Big-O
notation for a nested loop sorting algorithm

17
00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:25.214
indicates quadratic time complexity, O(n^2),
as input size doubles.

18
00:01:25.214 --> 00:01:33.507
NARRATOR: On screen, show a line graph with
input size on the x-axis and resource units on

19
00:01:33.507 --> 00:01:38.385
the y-axis, highlighting a quadratic curve
labeled with O(n^2) notation.

20
00:01:38.385 --> 00:01:45.053
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

21
00:01:45.053 --> 00:01:47.276
evidence would distinguish them.

22
00:01:47.276 --> 00:01:53.917
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

23
00:01:54.026 --> 00:01:59.968
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Loop Invariants for Correctness

24
00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:02.444
operates in a concrete sequence.

25
00:02:02.444 --> 00:02:09.116
NARRATOR: A loop invariant supports a proof by
initialization, maintenance, and termination.

26
00:02:09.116 --> 00:02:15.298
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: In a
linear search, the invariant states that the

27
00:02:15.298 --> 00:02:21.038
target element, if present, must be in the
unexamined portion of the array.

28
00:02:21.038 --> 00:02:27.474
NARRATOR: On screen, animate an array with a
moving boundary line, highlighting the

29
00:02:27.474 --> 00:02:31.929
unexamined segment and labeling it with the
invariant statement.

30
00:02:31.929 --> 00:02:39.210
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

31
00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:44.960
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

32
00:02:45.074 --> 00:02:51.708
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Divide-and-Conquer Strategy and make the

33
00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.367
decision criteria explicit.

34
00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:59.695
NARRATOR: Divide-and-conquer algorithms split
a problem, solve subproblems, and combine

35
00:02:59.695 --> 00:03:00.961
their results.

36
00:03:00.961 --> 00:03:07.946
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Merge
sort divides an array into halves, recursively

37
00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:14.930
sorts each half, and merges the sorted
subarrays into a single ordered array.

38
00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:21.768
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a recursive
tree diagram where each node splits into two

39
00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:26.164
children, with merge steps shown at the bottom
level.

40
00:03:26.164 --> 00:03:32.839
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

41
00:03:32.839 --> 00:03:34.508
move the goalposts.

42
00:03:34.508 --> 00:03:41.508
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

43
00:03:41.618 --> 00:03:48.246
NARRATOR: Apply Recurrence Relations to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

44
00:03:48.246 --> 00:03:49.193
isolated definition.

45
00:03:49.193 --> 00:03:53.943
NARRATOR: Recurrences describe the cost of
recursive algorithms.

46
00:03:53.943 --> 00:04:04.351
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) describes the

47
00:04:04.351 --> 00:04:11.786
time complexity of merge sort, solved via the
Master Theorem.

48
00:04:11.786 --> 00:04:19.486
NARRATOR: On screen, display a mathematical
recurrence equation alongside a step-by-step

49
00:04:19.486 --> 00:04:25.786
expansion showing how T(n) breaks down into
smaller subproblems.

50
00:04:25.786 --> 00:04:32.658
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

51
00:04:32.658 --> 00:04:34.130
at each step.

52
00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.458
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

53
00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:45.465
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Graph
Representation, including what improves and

54
00:04:45.465 --> 00:04:47.606
what becomes more difficult.

55
00:04:47.606 --> 00:04:53.865
NARRATOR: Graphs can be represented with
adjacency structures for traversal and path

56
00:04:53.865 --> 00:04:54.387
algorithms.

57
00:04:54.387 --> 00:05:01.128
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: An
adjacency list for a directed graph stores

58
00:05:01.128 --> 00:05:08.387
each vertex followed by a list of its outgoing
edges, enabling efficient neighbor lookups.

59
00:05:08.387 --> 00:05:15.632
NARRATOR: On screen, render a directed graph
with nodes and arrows, then overlay an

60
00:05:15.632 --> 00:05:21.324
adjacency list table showing each vertex
mapped to its connected neighbors.

61
00:05:21.324 --> 00:05:27.639
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

62
00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:28.090
acceptable.

63
00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:35.324
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

64
00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:42.589
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Graph Traversal Methods with an edge case and

65
00:05:42.589 --> 00:05:45.884
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

66
00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:53.806
NARRATOR: Graph algorithms operate on vertices
and edges to answer reachability, ordering,

67
00:05:53.806 --> 00:05:56.446
connectivity, and path questions.

68
00:05:56.446 --> 00:06:02.074
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Breadth-first search explores all vertices at

69
00:06:02.074 --> 00:06:09.747
a given depth before moving to the next level,
ensuring the shortest path in unweighted

70
00:06:09.747 --> 00:06:10.259
graphs.

71
00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:17.059
NARRATOR: On screen, animate a queue-based
traversal where vertices are visited level by

72
00:06:17.059 --> 00:06:22.290
level, with distance markers indicating path
length from the source.

73
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:28.510
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

74
00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:28.899
holds.

75
00:06:28.899 --> 00:06:35.271
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

76
00:06:35.271 --> 00:06:36.181
core idea.

77
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:42.932
NARRATOR: Use Data Structure Selection to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

78
00:06:42.932 --> 00:06:45.146
causes, and corrective action.

79
00:06:45.146 --> 00:06:50.518
NARRATOR: Representation choices and
invariants determine which operations are

80
00:06:50.518 --> 00:06:54.099
efficient and which errors are possible.

81
00:06:54.099 --> 00:07:01.833
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Choosing
a hash table over a linked list for key-value

82
00:07:01.833 --> 00:07:09.052
storage reduces lookup time from O(n) to O(1)
average case, provided a good collision

83
00:07:09.052 --> 00:07:10.599
